<%# HOW ONE POOL IS DOING, IN THE APP'S ONE ROW VOCABULARY (spec §4.4). Local: `row` — anything
    answering #status, #period_closed?, #changed_after_distributing?, #needs_attention?,
    #due_marker? and #balance_clause?. Today: `HomePresenter::Row`, `BudgetPagePresenter::Group`
    and `CategoryBudgetPresenter` (which serves BOTH of the category page's two blocks).

    ** WHAT IS NOT ON THIS PATH, SAID HERE RATHER THAN LEFT TO BE DISCOVERED. ** An undisclosed
    exception is how the next drift starts; a disclosed one is a design.

    THIS LIST WAS ITSELF INACCURATE AND THAT COST A REAL DEFECT (2d whole-plan review, fix 3). It
    claimed two callers stood outside; `grep -rn "pool_status_label(" app/` finds EIGHT call sites
    outside this file, across five files, and one of the four this header did not name was a
    genuine two-screens drift — see the alerts band below. A disclosure nobody re-greps is a
    comment, not a design. The list is now exhaustive against that grep, and re-running it is the
    whole maintenance instruction.

    ONE EXCEPTION, and it COMPOSES the label into a longer sentence, which is the one thing a span
    cannot do:

      - `HomeHelper#pool_problem_label` (Home's attention band) forces `period_closed:` internally
        rather than accepting it, deliberately, so that no caller of the band can omit it. Forcing
        it through here would mean giving this partial a composition seam so that one caller can
        wrap the output, which buys nothing and costs the property the file exists for. (Its
        `orphan:` keyword and the two clauses it wrapped the label in are DELETED with the concept
        — Task 6.)

      - `home/_standing.html.erb`'s overdraft line was a second and it is GONE: it labelled an
        ACCOUNT through `pool_status_label`, and Task 6 replaced it with the figure said plainly.
        An account holds no rules, so there was never a state for a status to read — only a
        balance, which the sentence now prints itself.

      - `categories/_partials/show/_budget_card` was a third and it is GONE: it hand-threaded both
        suffixes four inches above the pool card, on one page, about one envelope. Two callers of
        one method on one screen is the exact shape the whole-plan review found between Home's two
        bands, and `CategoryBudgetPresenter` already answered every question asked here, so it was
        converted rather than documented.

    THE DISTRIBUTION AND REALLOCATION SCREENS ARE NOT EXCEPTIONS, and there are two families of
    them rather than the one this header used to claim:

      - THE ROWS — `DistributionsHelper#distribution_line_detail`, `allocations/new`'s candidate
        list, and `distributions/_waterfall`'s alerts band — pass `period_closed:` and deliberately
        NOT the other suffix (see `HomeHelper#pool_status_label`), because their rows describe a
        move that has not happened yet. The alerts band passed neither until
        `DistributionPresenter::Standing` grew a `period_closed` member to carry it; that value
        object is designed to hold exactly the readers this vocabulary consumes, and the vocabulary
        grew after it was written. (The band could not actually be caught saying the shorter
        sentence — a closed envelope always asks for its swept rate again, so it always has a row
        and never reaches that band. Fixed anyway: an agreement that rests on another class's fill
        semantics is the kind this file exists to replace with one that rests on construction.)
      - THE PROJECTED STANDINGS — `DistributionsHelper#distribution_unrecoverable_sentence` and
        `AllocationsHelper`'s damage and gain sentences ("… becomes overdue · was Mar 1") — label
        a state that DOES NOT EXIST YET, inside a sentence, about a balance with the proposed
        movement already in it. Neither suffix, and neither is owed: "last period" would date a
        world nobody has written, and a rule edited since the last split is not what the sentence
        is about.

    WHY THIS PARTIAL EXISTS, AND IT IS NOT DE-DUPLICATION. `pool_status_label` takes BOTH suffixes
    as optional keywords, so every caller of it is free to thread one and forget the other, and a
    forgotten suffix is silent — the row still renders, just describing the same envelope
    differently from the screen next to it. That defect shipped twice: once between Home and
    /budget (`budget_page/_pool_group`, since renamed `_category_group`, passed `period_closed:`
    alone) and once between Home's OWN two bands
    (`pool_problem_label` passed `changed_after_distributing:` alone). Both were caught in review,
    neither by a test, because there is nothing to fail — the assertion has to already suspect the
    missing clause.

    So the threading happens ONCE, here, off ONE object. A caller no longer chooses which suffixes
    to pass; it chooses which OBJECT to pass, and an object that cannot answer all four questions
    raises NoMethodError the first time the row renders. That is the difference between "currently
    consistent" and "structurally hard to drift".

    THE INDENTATION BELOW IS LOAD-BEARING and deliberately does not read as nested markup. This
    partial replaced the identical span that stood in `home/_holding_row` (then `home/_pool_row`) and
    `budget_page/_pool_group`, and the refactor's contract was byte-identical rendering on both
    screens — so the literal whitespace those two files emitted lives here now, at the column each
    caller's own render line puts it. THOSE TWO render this from four spaces in; that is why the
    closing tag sits at four and the contents at six, and why moving either of them changes what
    this file has to say. There is no trailing newline for the same reason: the caller's own line
    supplies it.

    The category page's two callers are NOT byte-contracted, so they render this from wherever
    their markup sits and simply get source indentation that does not match their surroundings —
    invisible in the rendered page, and the honest cost of pinning the whitespace to the two
    screens whose bytes were measured.

    THE CLAUSE AFTER THE STATE IS THE ROW'S, not this file's. Home adds a date, the Budget page
    adds `· holds $X`, and neither is an accident of markup — a Home row shows no rules and no
    balance anywhere else on the line, while a Budget card lists every rule beneath it and is
    missing only the money. The row says which it wants; this partial does not ask which screen it
    is on. %>
<span class="text-sm <%= row.needs_attention? ? "text-status-danger font-medium" : "text-gray-700" %>">
      <%= pool_status_label(row.status,
                            period_closed: row.period_closed?,
                            changed_after_distributing: row.changed_after_distributing?) %>
<% if row.due_marker? %>        · <%= row.status.due_on.strftime("%b %-d") %>
<% end %><% if row.balance_clause? %>      <span class="text-gray-500"><%= pool_balance_clause(row.status) %></span>
<% end %>    </span>